  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back to check them.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" covers the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index, and its time field is `timestamp`. The fields include `response` / `response.keyword` (the HTTP response code, stored as text), `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `agent`, `extension`, `tags` and `utc_time`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-750c18b0 Web logs\" (id `s4-750c18b0-web-logs-1dd18a279744`) has exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests over `timestamp`.\n   - The response-code panel: a bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value, showing the top 20 codes.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-750c18b0 5xx errors\" (id `f7c673ad-08c1-49fd-9ebd-51782c0a7dda`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive within 5 minutes. It was created already enabled.\n   - Because the response code is stored as text, the rule compares codes alphabetically. That works for normal three-digit codes, but a code with an unusual length (such as \"5000\") would also be counted.\n4. **Edit and check:** The response-code panel's title is now \"Responses by code\". Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
